 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Wood and cotton cloth are made mainly of cellulose (carbon, hydrogen and oxygen). When they burn in air:\nwood + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water (+ ash and smoke)\n• The ash is the mineral part that does not burn.\n• Smoke is tiny unburnt carbon particles; it shows burning is not complete.\n• Heating wood without air (as when charcoal is made) drives off gases and leaves charcoal (carbon).\nSynthetic cloth (nylon, polyester) melts and drips as it burns and can stick to skin; cotton burns with a smell of burning paper and leaves a soft ash.\nSafety: do not wear loose synthetic clothes near open fires; if clothes catch fire: stop, drop and roll.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "x": "someone's clothes catch fire"
  },
  "problem": "What should be done if {x}?",
  "steps": [
   "Running fans the flames with more air.",
   "Rolling on the ground smothers the flames (cuts off oxygen)."
  ],
  "answer": "Stop, drop and roll (or wrap the person in a blanket).",
  "checks": []
 },
